{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when Elohim caused me to wander.</b> Onkelos translated what he translated. And there is yet another matter to settle according to its plain sense: when the Holy One, blessed be He, brought me out of my father's house to wander and drift from place to place, I knew that I would pass through a place of wicked men — <b>that is the kindness you must do me:</b>" "<b>When they caused me to wander.</b> Plural. And do not wonder, for there are many places where a term of divinity and a term of mastery is called in the plural: ״אֲשֶׁר הָלְכוּ אֱלֹהִים״ (II Samuel 7), ״אֱלֹהִים חַיִּים״ (Deuteronomy 5), ״אֱלֹהִים קְדֹשִׁים״ (Joshua 24), and every term of lordship is in the plural — thus ״וַיִּקַּח אֲדֹנֵי יוֹסֵף״ (Genesis 39), ״וַאֲדֹנֵי הָאֲדֹנִים״ (Deuteronomy 10), ״אֲדֹנֵי הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 42), and likewise ״בְּעָלָיו עִמּוֹ״ (Exodus 22), ״וְהוּעַד בִּבְעָלָיו״ (ibid. 21). And if you say: what is the meaning of הִתְעוּ? — all who are exiled from their place and are not settled are called תּוֹעֶה, as in ״וַתֵּלֶךְ וַתֵּתַע״ (Genesis 21), ״תָּעִיתִי כְּשֶׂה אֹבֵד״ (Psalms 119), ״יִתְעוּ לִבְלִי אֹכֶל״ (Job 38) — they went out and wandered to seek food." "<b>Say of me.</b> About me. And likewise ״וַיִּשְׁאֲלוּ אַנְשֵׁי הַמָּקוֹם לְאִשְׁתּוֹ״ (Genesis 26) — about his wife; and likewise ״וְאָמַר פַּרְעֹה לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 14) — about the children of Israel; ״פֶּן יֹאמְרוּ לִי אִשָּׁה הֲרָגָתְהוּ״ (Judges 9)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:45.908790360Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}